WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.500
The One Where MVP walkthrough — Speak a memory, find the photo, and save
the words that make it easier to retrieve next time. This walkthrough

00:05.500 --> 00:11.000
starts with voice input in Chrome.

00:11.000 --> 00:15.500
Four places to start — Library lets you browse. Find searches from
remembered fragments. Card invites a memory note. Index shows the phrases

00:15.500 --> 00:20.000
saved against photos.

00:20.000 --> 00:26.000
Voice search works in Chrome — Voice is a core MVP feature: speak a memory
instead of typing it. It works in Chrome. Use Chrome for this demo because

00:26.000 --> 00:32.000
Brave does not support the voice flow.

00:32.000 --> 00:38.000
Say what you remember — Tap the microphone, allow access if prompted, and
say a fragment such as “a café with yellow walls and green chairs.” Review

00:38.000 --> 00:44.000
the recognised words, then send with the arrow.

00:44.000 --> 00:48.500
Speak a postcard note too — Card also offers voice input. Reveal the
photo, tap its microphone, and say the memory you want to save. A spoken

00:48.500 --> 00:53.000
note can become a cue for finding that photo again.

00:53.000 --> 00:57.500
See what can be found — The curated demo contains 37 items: 30 photos,
five receipts, and two screenshots, spanning 2019–2024. The count

00:57.500 --> 01:02.000
describes this demo library.

01:02.000 --> 01:12.500
Browse, open, inspect — Tap a thumbnail to open its detail sheet. Close it
to continue browsing. Scroll to explore more of the library.

01:12.500 --> 01:16.500
More than trip photos — Receipts and screenshots appear alongside everyday
photos. Type badges make documents and screenshots easier to spot in the

01:16.500 --> 01:20.500
grid.

01:20.500 --> 01:25.000
Read the photo context — The detail sheet shows the description, date,
location, people, and item kind when that information is available. Close

01:25.000 --> 01:29.500
returns to the grid.

01:29.500 --> 01:33.500
Inspect a receipt — A receipt opens in the same detail sheet. You can
inspect the original image and its date, location, and receipt

01:33.500 --> 01:37.500
classification.

01:37.500 --> 01:41.500
Jump from browsing to search — “Search for this one” opens Find with a
short description and submits it. You can continue describing the item in

01:41.500 --> 01:45.500
your own words.

01:45.500 --> 01:50.000
Start with a fragment — Choose a suggested search or type anything you
remember: a colour, person, object, expression, or approximate context.

01:50.000 --> 01:54.500
Use the arrow to send it.

01:54.500 --> 02:00.850
Describe it naturally — Here, a café search starts with yellow walls and
green chairs. The MVP processes the description, shows candidate photos,

02:00.850 --> 02:07.200
and asks a follow-up question.

02:07.200 --> 02:11.700
Ask a useful next question — The expression-based example asks whether
Priya was holding her chest. The follow-up gives the user another way to

02:11.700 --> 02:16.200
distinguish similar frames.

02:16.200 --> 02:20.200
Show uncertainty visibly — Similar photos are grouped as the same moment.
The MVP explicitly says it cannot tell them apart from the information it

02:20.200 --> 02:24.200
has.

02:24.200 --> 02:28.700
Add another remembered clue — A follow-up can add who was there or what
the chairs looked like. The conversation returns another candidate set and

02:28.700 --> 02:33.200
can suggest asking someone who was there.

02:33.200 --> 02:39.200
Compare three modes — Switch between Classic Search, Single answer, and
The One Where. Classic uses a keyword result grid; Single answer selects

02:39.200 --> 02:45.200
one; The One Where supports follow-up questions.

02:45.200 --> 02:53.200
Classic: a keyword grid — A simple “cafe” keyword returns 21 items in this
demo. The user scans thumbnails and opens whichever result looks right.

02:53.200 --> 02:58.200
Classic: no keyword match — The longer, vague description returned no
keyword match. “Try describing it instead” was visible; clicking it did

02:58.200 --> 03:03.200
not visibly change the mode in the captured run.

03:03.200 --> 03:07.700
Single answer: one selection — Single answer responds with an explanation
and one selected photo. The viewer can still open that photo and confirm

03:07.700 --> 03:12.200
whether it is the intended one.

03:12.200 --> 03:16.700
An honest no-match response — The clinic-receipt suggestion did not match
an item in the curated library. The MVP said it could not find that clinic

03:16.700 --> 03:21.200
receipt or amount.

03:21.200 --> 03:25.700
Open the selected photo — The full-size viewer shows the photo and its
available context: date, place, and people. The close control returns to

03:25.700 --> 03:30.200
the search.

03:30.200 --> 03:34.700
Tell the MVP: “That’s it” — Confirming the photo saves the words used
during the search as cues for that image. “View your index” leads to the

03:34.700 --> 03:39.200
saved memory phrases.

03:39.200 --> 03:44.200
Ask someone who was there — An “Ask them?” suggestion opens Pass It On.
This is a two-device simulation: the left pane is your search; the right

03:44.200 --> 03:49.200
pane demonstrates Amit’s reply.

03:49.200 --> 03:54.200
Give the other person a clue — “Ask Amit” reveals a distinguishing
question. The café scenario offers a cold coffee, a snack jar, or the

03:54.200 --> 03:59.200
table edge, plus a free-text answer field.

03:59.200 --> 04:04.200
Keep both recollections — The café demo adds Amit’s reply and saves it as
a deposit. It also explains the blue-versus-green chair disagreement

04:04.200 --> 04:09.200
instead of declaring one memory wrong.

04:09.200 --> 04:13.700
Try the stalled-trek scenario — The scenario buttons switch the demo. A
stalled trek search asks about the time of reaching the summit signboard

04:13.700 --> 04:18.200
to help distinguish similar photos.

04:18.200 --> 04:22.200
A reply can be free text — The recipient can type a remembered detail
rather than use a preset. The demo displays that reply back in the

04:22.200 --> 04:26.200
original search conversation.

04:26.200 --> 04:31.200
Use the shared memory again — Selecting the mid-morning clue adds it to
the conversation, updates the candidate emphasis, and saves the reply as a

04:31.200 --> 04:36.200
deposit for the photo.

04:36.200 --> 04:40.200
A small reason to revisit — Card presents a hidden, forgotten photo. The
scratch coating turns opening the memory into an interaction, with “Not

04:40.200 --> 04:44.200
this one” available to skip it.

04:44.200 --> 04:49.200
Scratch to reveal — Drag across the coating. The image gradually becomes
visible as the scratched area grows.

04:49.200 --> 04:57.200
Skip a photo you do not want — “Not this one” loads a replacement card.
The new image begins hidden, ready for another reveal.

04:57.200 --> 05:00.200
Reveal the next memory — Continue scratching until the coating disappears.
The memory prompt and message field then become visible beneath the photo.

05:00.200 --> 05:04.200
The prompt is now ready — The revealed card asks “What was this?” Type or
speak a short phrase that carries personal context the photo alone may not

05:04.200 --> 05:08.200
contain. Use Chrome for voice input.

05:08.200 --> 05:12.700
Write the memory in your words — For this demo, the sample note is “The
cinnamon joke at our coffee catch-up.” Pressing the arrow attaches the

05:12.700 --> 05:17.200
phrase to this photo.

05:17.200 --> 05:21.700
Make the payoff immediate — The card confirms that the photo now answers
to the saved phrase. “Try finding it” opens Find and searches that phrase

05:21.700 --> 05:26.200
immediately.

05:26.200 --> 05:31.200
Retrieve the same photo — The saved phrase retrieves this photo. The
result quotes the note and carries a “YOUR WORDS” badge, showing the

05:31.200 --> 05:36.200
connection to the user’s memory cue.

05:36.200 --> 05:44.200
One card for today — Returning after completion shows “You’ve opened
today’s card.” The next-card message says another will arrive tomorrow.

05:44.200 --> 05:49.700
See the phrases you have saved — Index groups remembered phrases under
their photos, with available photo context and the note date. It brings

05:49.700 --> 05:55.200
together confirmed search words, card notes, and handoff deposits.

05:55.200 --> 05:59.700
Move beyond the curated demo — “Use your own photos” reveals the Demo
Library / Your Photos switch. Selecting Your Photos opens the notice and

05:59.700 --> 06:04.200
the photo picker.

06:04.200 --> 06:09.700
Read the upload notice — The notice says images are resized in the browser
and kept locally, while downscaled copies are sent for description. It

06:09.700 --> 06:15.200
also notes possible Google model-improvement use.

06:15.200 --> 06:19.700
Choose 10–60 images — The picker accepts multiple photos. This walkthrough
used ten images already present in the public demo library, rather than

06:19.700 --> 06:24.200
private personal photos.

06:24.200 --> 06:29.200
Wait while descriptions are created — The upload run paused at “Waiting
for API limits…” during image description. The waiting time is shortened

06:29.200 --> 06:34.200
here; all ten images later finished indexing.

06:34.200 --> 06:39.200
Inspect the indexed library — All ten selected images appear in Your
photos with generated descriptions. In this run, missing dates caused them

06:39.200 --> 06:44.200
to be grouped as a same-day set.

06:44.200 --> 06:49.700
Dates depend on the source file — The site warned about missing EXIF
dates. This sample detail sheet shows 7 October 2026 as its date, so that

06:49.700 --> 06:55.200
displayed date should not be treated as the original capture date.

06:55.200 --> 07:00.200
Search your uploaded collection — A description of a woman in green, iced
coffee, and yellow café walls finds a matching uploaded image. The header

07:00.200 --> 07:05.200
makes the active collection explicit.

07:05.200 --> 07:09.700
Confirm and save a cue here too — Uploaded results use the same viewer and
“That’s it” flow. Confirmation saves the search words against the uploaded

07:09.700 --> 07:14.200
image and provides an Index shortcut.

07:14.200 --> 07:18.700
A control for restarting the demo — The Card header includes “Reset demo
state.” It is shown here for completeness; the reset action was not

07:18.700 --> 07:23.200
executed during the walkthrough.

07:23.200 --> 07:27.700
Delete the uploaded collection — The Your Photos notice includes “Delete
everything.” The control is shown, but deletion was not executed, so its

07:27.700 --> 07:32.200
final outcome is not demonstrated.

07:32.200 --> 07:37.700
From a fragment to a memory cue — Browse the collection, describe a
fragment, refine or ask someone, confirm the photo, and add personal words

07:37.700 --> 07:43.200
that make the next search easier.
